WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.310
When it comes to making movies, actors are
just one small piece of a huge puzzle.

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:08.780
Between the writing, the directing, the editing,
and the marketing, there are so many things

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:13.059
that can go wrong that it's no wonder stars
occasionally think their own movies are going

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:14.650
to be total bombs.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.880
Sometimes they're right, but other times,
stars have been shocked when their films actually

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.980
became huge hits instead.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.700
Here's a look at some insanely successful
films that even their stars didn't believe

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:25.980
in.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.650
The Terminator

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:32.250
When Arnold Schwarzenegger was offered the
title role in the 1984 sci-fi classic The

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.850
Terminator, his agent told him it would be
the end of his career.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:39.760
But Schwarzenegger disagreed, not because
he thought the movie was going to be good,

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.679
but because he figured the film was so "low
profile" it wouldn't even register as a blip

00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:45.229
on his career when it failed.

00:00:45.229 --> 00:00:49.649
Schwarzenegger remained just as negative as
he was gearing up to film the movie, telling

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:54.190
a friend who inquired about a strange pair
of boots in his trailer on the set of Conan

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:58.289
the Destroyer that they were from "some s---y
movie I'm doing."

00:00:58.289 --> 00:01:02.850
Of course, the boots were part of his costume
for The Terminator, you know, the film that

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.009
made him a household name.

00:01:05.009 --> 00:01:06.700
The Full Monty

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:11.310
If you've never seen The Full Monty, just
imagine Magic Mike, only filmed in a bleak

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:16.280
English city in the '90s, and instead of shredded
American actors, starred a bunch of English

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.009
guys with dad bods.

00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:23.340
The film was a box office smash, earned four
Oscar nominations, and is consistently ranked

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.159
as one of the greatest British films ever
made.

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:29.180
Also, lead actor Robert Carlyle hated it.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.180
Why?

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.960
Well, in an interview with talk show host
Graham Norton, Carlyle explained that the

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.619
shoot was extremely hard, and that the first
cut of the movie was so bad executives planned

00:01:38.619 --> 00:01:39.939
to send it straight to video.

00:01:39.939 --> 00:01:42.579
"It was a tough shoot, Full Monty was a tough
shoot."

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:44.899
"You really didn't see 'hit' written on Full
Monty?"

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.000
"Thought it was a load of s---."

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.900
[Laughter]

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:54.140
Luckily, after the director was fired, a new
editor was brought in who totally recut the

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.060
film, resulting in the classic version fans
know and love.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.380
Nice.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:00.909
It

00:02:00.909 --> 00:02:05.820
With near-universal critical acclaim and hundreds
of millions of dollars made at the box office,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.660
It ranks among the most critically and commercially
successful horror movies ever made.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:15.319
And that was a huge relief to star Bill Skarsgård,
who told Variety he was convinced the whole

00:02:15.319 --> 00:02:17.799
time that the movie was going to fail, saying,

00:02:17.799 --> 00:02:23.150
"I just felt something really terrible was
going to happen to me personally, or the movie."

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:28.060
Not because he thought it was bad, but because
he thought things were going too well, and

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.110
kept waiting for the other shoe to drop.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:35.130
It never did, though, and the movie became
one of the biggest smash hits of 2017.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.599
Pirates of the Caribbean

00:02:36.599 --> 00:02:41.069
Nobody expected Pirates of the Caribbean:
The Curse of the Black Pearl to succeed.

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:46.930
It was a PG-13 Disney pirate adventure based
on a theme park ride, just for starters.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:50.819
Even Disney head honcho Michael Eisner tried
to shut production down when he found out

00:02:50.819 --> 00:02:53.019
it was going to cost over $100 million.

00:02:53.019 --> 00:03:00.019
"That probably was the most difficult decision
in my history on committing to a movie."

00:03:00.019 --> 00:03:02.760
Eisner wasn't alone in his misgivings.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:07.300
During a frank interview with The Guardian
in 2008, Keira Knightley confessed that most

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.299
of the principal cast were embarrassed to
be working on the movie.

00:03:10.299 --> 00:03:14.599
"It was really f---ing embarrassing and we
all thought it was going to be total s---."

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:18.540
Knightley never told any of her friends she
was working on it, which must have made for

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:22.819
some fun conversations after it turned out
to be a massive worldwide hit that spawned

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:24.960
a blockbuster franchise.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.360
Guardians of the Galaxy

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:31.100
Like many in Hollywood, Chris Pratt was pretty
worried that the first Guardians of the Galaxy

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:33.420
movie was just too weird to succeed.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.750
In fact, both he and director James Gunn admitted
that they were pretty worried that their careers

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:43.260
would be over if, as pundits predicted, the
film turned out to be "Marvel's first bomb."

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:44.840
Pratt told The Hollywood Reporter,

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.530
"You don't make a movie with a talking tree.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:50.250
You don't spend this much money on a raccoon
with a gun.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:53.200
For me, I was like, 'Oh, so this movie is
going to bomb.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.200
Done.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.480
This is the end of my career.'"

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:01.260
More than $700 million and a heap of critical
praise later, Pratt was the face of a franchise,

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.260
and Gunn was one of Hollywood's hottest writer-directors.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.020
Pass the ammo, trash pandas!

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:08.020
Taken

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:13.859
Released when Liam Neeson was in his mid-50s,
Taken somehow managed to take a veteran actor

00:04:13.859 --> 00:04:19.030
known almost exclusively for his roles in
serious dramas and a single Star Wars movie

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:21.510
and establish him as an action star.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:26.510
It turned out to be a surprise box office
success that spawned a film trilogy and TV

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:30.600
spinoff, and nobody was more surprised than
Neeson himself.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:35.560
During a visit to The Late Late Show, Neeson
admitted that he figured the film's plot of

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:40.280
"a man punches half of Europe to find his
daughter" was too basic to warrant a cinematic

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.280
release.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.730
"I just thought, 'No, it's straight to video.'

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:45.840
It's like, it's simple."

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.730
Neeson reportedly took the role of ex-CIA
agent Bryan Mills because he was sure he'd

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:54.980
never get another offer to do an action movie,
considering his age.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:58.350
Nearly a decade later, he's still whooping
bad-guy butt.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:04:59.880
Animal House

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.530
According to Hollywood lore, during the production
of Animal House, an executive from Universal

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:09.540
approached director John Landis with a simple
ultimatum: find a big-name star they could

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.660
put on the poster, or the studio would pull
the plug.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.730
Luckily for Landis, he just so happened to
be good friends with Donald Sutherland, who

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:21.030
said that he'd happily appear in the movie,
for $250,000.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:28.070
The studio said no, so Landis made a counteroffer:
$25,000 for a single day's work and two percent

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:29.860
of the film's gross.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.340
Sutherland told Landis "I just want the money,"
because he didn't think the movie would succeed.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:37.960
So a revised offer of $35,000 was drawn up.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:43.770
By his own estimation, turning down that initial
two percent offer cost Sutherland $14 million

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:45.670
for a single day's work.

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:46.740
Whoops!

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:48.140
Star Wars

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:52.710
Even George Lucas, the mastermind behind the
Star Wars franchise, wasn't so sure the film

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:53.710
would be a hit.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:59.110
Shortly before Star Wars hit theaters in 1977,
he visited the set of his friend Steven Spielberg's

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:01.870
new movie, Close Encounters of the Third Kind.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:06.310
Lucas was so impressed he became convinced
Close Encounters was going to blow Star Wars

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:08.690
away at the box office.

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:13.810
Spielberg disagreed, and offered up a deal:
he would give Lucas 2.5% of Close Encounters'

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:18.810
box office gross in exchange for a 2.5% stake
in Star Wars.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:24.270
Lucas jumped at the deal - and by some estimates,
Spielberg ended up making nearly $40 million

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:25.680
as a result.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:28.180
Now that turned out to be a pretty good bet!

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:29.380
Thanks for watching!

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:31.870
